1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,840
A deadly disease swept through America.

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:07,000
It struck suddenly and mysteriously.

3
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:11,220
Thousands would die.

4
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:14,080
Many more would be left helpless.

5
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:17,440
This disease was terrifying.

6
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:19,880
We didn't know how it was transmitted.

7
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:23,640
We knew there was a virus, but we didn't know how we got it.

8
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:27,080
We didn't know how our kids got it, and we were terrified about it.

9
00:00:27,081 --> 00:00:33,680
It was frightening because it affected children, and it affected them in a particularly poignant way.

10
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:37,160
The disease was poliomyelitis.

11
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:43,680
Polio would enlist renowned scientists, shake the vision of one of the world's greatest leaders,

12
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:48,540
and transform a young unknown doctor into a world-famous hero.

13
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:54,480
Ingenuity, trial and error, even acts of scientific desperation

14
00:00:54,481 --> 00:00:58,560
would pave the way for one of the greatest breakthroughs in scientific history.

15
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:03,420
On Modern Marvels, the search for the polio vaccine.

16
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:38,480
As America entered the 1950s, a disease unheard of just 50 years earlier had grown into an

17
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:42,905
annual event of terror and apprehension.

18
00:01:42,906 --> 00:01:47,400
Polio targeted anyone at any age, but mostly it

19
00:01:47,401 --> 00:01:53,460
targeted young, healthy children, making it seem more destructive and cruel than other diseases.

20
00:01:53,461 --> 00:01:58,460
Polio, also called infantile paralysis, was a mystery.

21
00:02:00,460 --> 00:02:02,460
Did they get it from the water?

22
00:02:02,461 --> 00:02:03,460
Did they get it from insects?

23
00:02:03,461 --> 00:02:05,460
Did they get it from animals?

24
00:02:05,461 --> 00:02:07,460
We were petrified and many parents,

25
00:02:07,461 --> 00:02:09,460
many of us kept our kids home.

26
00:02:09,461 --> 00:02:11,460
We were frightened.

27
00:02:11,461 --> 00:02:12,460
We were frightened.

28
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:17,460
The lives of millions of children were changed

29
00:02:17,461 --> 00:02:20,460
by the fear polio caused.

30
00:02:20,461 --> 00:02:22,460
It was really frightening for parents,

31
00:02:22,461 --> 00:02:27,420
for parents during the polio years, there were terms such as polio weather.

32
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:32,412
Don't send the children to the beach. They can't go to the community pool.

33
00:02:32,413 --> 00:02:33,740
No one knew where polio

34
00:02:33,741 --> 00:02:37,689
came from. They didn't know how to protect their children.

35
00:02:37,690 --> 00:02:40,140
One day, scientists would learn that

36
00:02:40,141 --> 00:02:42,160
polio was a fecal-oral disease.

37
00:02:42,161 --> 00:02:46,460
Children or adults coming in close contact could infect each other

38
00:02:46,461 --> 00:02:49,284
through water or food contaminated by feces.

39
00:02:49,285 --> 00:02:52,300
But for the first half of the century, only the

40
00:02:52,301 --> 00:02:57,640
fearsome symptoms were understood. It began with a sniffle, then a fever, then

41
00:02:57,641 --> 00:03:03,460
suddenly the cries of a child screaming in fear, unable to move, sometimes unable

42
00:03:03,461 --> 00:03:08,740
to get a breath of air. Polio patients spent weeks in the hospital trying to

43
00:03:08,741 --> 00:03:14,360
battle the odds and recover. The diagnosis of polio was just the beginning of a

44
00:03:14,361 --> 00:03:20,940
long painful journey. One of the fears I think was that polio could not only

45
00:03:20,941 --> 00:03:24,420
obviously severely threatened the life of a child,

46
00:03:24,421 --> 00:03:27,440
but that even if the child survived,

47
00:03:27,441 --> 00:03:31,220
there would be some significant polio handicap,

48
00:03:32,620 --> 00:03:34,620
which would be the result of that virus.

49
00:03:35,780 --> 00:03:37,720
The virus itself lasted anywhere

50
00:03:37,721 --> 00:03:39,300
from two to four weeks,

51
00:03:39,301 --> 00:03:41,660
but the damage to the spine could shut down

52
00:03:41,661 --> 00:03:45,200
healthy muscles in arms, legs, even lungs.

53
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,300
Therapy began immediately after the fever subsided.

54
00:03:50,940 --> 00:03:54,940
Rehabilitation was long, tedious, and painful.

55
00:03:54,941 --> 00:04:05,220
One of the things that's not in a lot of the literature also is that polio has a lot of

56
00:04:05,221 --> 00:04:08,760
pain associated with it on a daily basis.

57
00:04:08,761 --> 00:04:13,080
Not a severe pain, but constant pain.

58
00:04:13,081 --> 00:04:20,460
And the pain is associated not so much with the handicap, but with the wearing of a brace.

59
00:04:20,461 --> 00:04:24,080
With the help of a brace, the fortunate might walk again.

60
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:29,140
The less fortunate were placed in iron lungs to help them breathe,

61
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:32,400
forever trapped in the humming metal tubes.

62
00:04:36,460 --> 00:04:41,660
Polio survivors could look forward to a life in a world ill-equipped for their special needs,

63
00:04:41,661 --> 00:04:44,780
a life of dependency and continual care.

64
00:04:44,781 --> 00:04:53,020
And my own family, they were kind of locked in to taking care of me.

65
00:04:53,021 --> 00:04:54,520
I was like the dishes.

66
00:04:54,521 --> 00:04:58,400
No, I felt as though I was treated like the dishes.

67
00:04:58,401 --> 00:05:01,540
It's your turn to give her exercises today.

68
00:05:01,541 --> 00:05:03,340
I gave her exercises yesterday.

69
00:05:03,341 --> 00:05:04,560
You know, let's switch.

70
00:05:04,561 --> 00:05:08,140
I'll do it today if you do it for me tomorrow, that kind of thing.

71
00:05:08,141 --> 00:05:13,540
Families were ill-equipped as well, and so were important public institutions.

72
00:05:13,541 --> 00:05:18,920
In my school, there were many times when I was just forgotten.

73
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:22,660
And I'll never forget the time I was left in the classroom.

74
00:05:23,340 --> 00:05:26,160
The teacher hadn't made any arrangement for me.

75
00:05:26,380 --> 00:05:32,780
And I sat in the room, this little child just crying silently

76
00:05:32,781 --> 00:05:36,140
because I didn't want to bother anybody.

77
00:05:36,940 --> 00:05:41,760
And I was just going to sit there, but no provision had been made for me.

78
00:05:41,761 --> 00:05:48,760
Often, polio meant a series of long, painful surgeries to free paralyzed limbs.

79
00:05:48,761 --> 00:05:52,760
Most polio patients have gone through several operations.

80
00:05:52,761 --> 00:06:01,760
And the thing with the operations was that they rehabilitated you to your optimum at one point.

81
00:06:01,761 --> 00:06:08,760
Then they did the operation and you lost all that ground and you had to restart all over again.

82
00:06:08,761 --> 00:06:12,560
And that was hard. That was really hard.

83
00:06:15,040 --> 00:06:18,500
Even the Egyptians battled the dreaded disease polio.

84
00:06:19,380 --> 00:06:24,000
But an epidemic striking thousands was a phenomenon of the 20th century.

85
00:06:24,720 --> 00:06:27,560
It was in 1916 that the horror had begun.

86
00:06:28,260 --> 00:06:37,220
America, preparing for World War I, had little idea that a devastating and destructive virus was about to attack at home.

87
00:06:37,221 --> 00:06:42,760
It was in the muggy heat of Manhattan in June that the first cases were reported.

88
00:06:43,580 --> 00:06:46,320
By July, a few cases had grown to 100.

89
00:06:47,100 --> 00:06:50,680
By September, over 1,000 new cases had appeared.

90
00:06:52,140 --> 00:06:54,360
Health officials and doctors were baffled.

91
00:06:55,220 --> 00:06:56,140
Why polio?

92
00:06:56,940 --> 00:06:57,960
Why now?

93
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:05,780
When the contagion subsided, over 27,000 children had been infected,

94
00:07:05,781 --> 00:07:08,000
all in just under five months.

95
00:07:09,400 --> 00:07:10,360
Many would recover.

96
00:07:10,740 --> 00:07:12,320
Polio wasn't always fatal.

97
00:07:12,321 --> 00:07:16,860
But 2,000 were paralyzed, and over 2,000 had died.

98
00:07:17,440 --> 00:07:20,760
Astounding numbers for a disease that was considered rare.

99
00:07:22,740 --> 00:07:27,260
From the beginning, the questions mounted faster than the answers.

100
00:07:27,960 --> 00:07:30,500
The little that was known wasn't very encouraging.

101
00:07:30,501 --> 00:07:37,740
The major challenge in medicine and in trying to prevent disease and control disease is

102
00:07:37,741 --> 00:07:41,220
to find the causes of the disease and how it evolves.

103
00:07:41,221 --> 00:07:52,840
We did not know what caused poliomyelitis, so we could not prevent the disease.

104
00:07:52,841 --> 00:07:57,400
Even if the cause of polio wasn't known, early medical pioneers had worked to discover

105
00:07:57,401 --> 00:08:00,840
the secrets of other deadly viruses.

106
00:08:00,841 --> 00:08:07,340
In 1796, Edward Jenner experimented with a killer virus and gave the world its first

107
00:08:07,341 --> 00:08:10,340
vaccine.

108
00:08:10,341 --> 00:08:14,940
Smallpox was considered a major scorch, had destroyed civilizations.

109
00:08:14,941 --> 00:08:23,580
So Edward Jenner observed that milkmaids that used to milk the cows would not get smallpox.

110
00:08:23,581 --> 00:08:30,960
That observation led him to believe that individuals infected with cowpox would become immune against

111
00:08:30,961 --> 00:08:32,340
smallpox.

112
00:08:32,341 --> 00:08:38,920
So Jenner used the cowpox to protect individuals against smallpox.

113
00:08:38,921 --> 00:08:46,380
The Latin word for cow is vaca, so Jenner called his method a vaccination.

114
00:08:46,381 --> 00:08:51,960
Louis Pasteur, almost 100 years later, would provide the next vital link in understanding

115
00:08:51,961 --> 00:08:53,520
immunization.

116
00:08:53,521 --> 00:08:58,960
Working with rabies, Pasteur believed, like Jenner, that the disease itself could be used

117
00:08:58,961 --> 00:09:01,900
to fight the infection it caused.

118
00:09:01,901 --> 00:09:06,620
But rather than inject the blood directly, this time Pasteur would dilute or attenuate

119
00:09:06,621 --> 00:09:08,180
the rabies virus.

120
00:09:08,181 --> 00:09:13,080
He reasoned correctly that a weakened virus would still cause the body to fight the infection

121
00:09:13,081 --> 00:09:15,320
without causing the illness.

122
00:09:15,321 --> 00:09:19,900
Pasteur diluted rabies in the spinal cord tissue of animals, then ground the tissue

123
00:09:19,901 --> 00:09:21,960
and injected it into humans.

124
00:09:21,961 --> 00:09:24,660
was a risky, sometimes deadly, procedure.

125
00:09:24,661 --> 00:09:28,940
Sometimes it worked, but it was very controversial.

126
00:09:28,941 --> 00:09:31,080
Pester, like many creative scientists,

127
00:09:31,081 --> 00:09:34,240
went against many of the traditions of the day.

128
00:09:34,241 --> 00:09:38,460
And so he endured criticism, as many creative scientists

129
00:09:38,461 --> 00:09:40,960
and people in other fields would.

130
00:09:40,961 --> 00:09:42,900
In the 19th century, scientists

131
00:09:42,901 --> 00:09:45,180
worked to solve the polio puzzle.

132
00:09:45,181 --> 00:09:47,140
But progress was slow.

133
00:09:47,141 --> 00:09:49,340
Many times, those diseases were supposed

134
00:09:49,341 --> 00:09:52,640
to be coming from fumes from the environment, you know,

135
00:09:52,641 --> 00:09:56,540
and from the positions of the stars and so on and so forth.

136
00:09:58,040 --> 00:10:01,020
One of the major challenges in the prevention of a disease

137
00:10:01,021 --> 00:10:03,560
is to find out what provokes that disease.

138
00:10:03,561 --> 00:10:06,520
In 1908, scientist Carl Landensteiner

139
00:10:06,521 --> 00:10:09,520
proved what many scientists had suspected.

140
00:10:09,521 --> 00:10:12,460
Polio was caused by a virus.

141
00:10:12,461 --> 00:10:14,400
So it was the first breakthrough.

142
00:10:14,401 --> 00:10:18,640
Okay, now we can see if through manipulation

143
00:10:18,641 --> 00:10:23,440
of this virus in a way or another, eventually we can have a vaccine.

144
00:10:23,441 --> 00:10:27,280
But knowing that a virus caused polio was one thing.

145
00:10:27,281 --> 00:10:31,080
Finding a way to vaccinate against it was another.

146
00:10:31,081 --> 00:10:33,680
By the early 1920s, outbreaks of polio

147
00:10:33,681 --> 00:10:35,600
were becoming more frequent.

148
00:10:35,601 --> 00:10:39,200
Polio dominated the headlines during summer months.

149
00:10:39,201 --> 00:10:44,200
And America was about to learn that even the mighty weren't immune.

150
00:10:48,640 --> 00:10:58,320
As America danced its way into the 1920s, the nation's optimism and sense of youthful

151
00:10:58,321 --> 00:11:02,600
fun was tempered by a creeping illness that could strike at any moment.

152
00:11:03,440 --> 00:11:07,420
Polio was taking more lives and crippling more children every year.

153
00:11:08,140 --> 00:11:11,120
Although it preferred children, adults were not immune.

154
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:17,600
Franklin Roosevelt was a young and ambitious star in New York politics.

155
00:11:18,360 --> 00:11:25,740
Cousin Teddy had paved the way, and Franklin, patrician, wealthy, was poised to carry on the Roosevelt legacy.

156
00:11:26,620 --> 00:11:33,120
But polio would create a detour in his political life, a fortunate accident for the fight against the disease.

157
00:11:34,320 --> 00:11:39,120
In 1920, Franklin had just finished a grueling campaign for vice president.

158
00:11:39,680 --> 00:11:43,020
He lost, but was considered a contender for the presidency.

159
00:11:43,940 --> 00:11:46,360
At 38, he was poised for greatness.

160
00:11:46,980 --> 00:11:49,880
All he had to do was step up to accept the mantle.

161
00:11:52,600 --> 00:11:55,060
Tired by his vice presidential campaign,

162
00:11:55,400 --> 00:11:58,420
Roosevelt retired to Campobello, his family retreat.

163
00:11:59,320 --> 00:12:02,480
On the first day, he swam and went fishing with his children.

164
00:12:03,280 --> 00:12:06,020
On the second day, he felt a little feverish.

165
00:12:06,800 --> 00:12:11,600
On the morning of the third day, he fell to the floor, unable to get up.

166
00:12:11,601 --> 00:12:19,540
Fortunately, a doctor was vacationing nearby, but the diagnosis was devastating.

167
00:12:20,540 --> 00:12:21,380
It was polio.

168
00:12:26,540 --> 00:12:30,020
Roosevelt easily could have retired into a life of luxury.

169
00:12:30,580 --> 00:12:32,960
Instead, he chose to fight the disease.

170
00:12:33,800 --> 00:12:37,700
Roosevelt was desperately trying to regain the ability to walk.

171
00:12:38,380 --> 00:12:41,680
And in 1924, he went down to Warm Springs, Georgia,

172
00:12:41,800 --> 00:12:45,040
because he heard that there were warm waters and a pool at the spa there.

173
00:12:45,400 --> 00:12:48,440
He thought that swimming in the warm water and moving in the warm water

174
00:12:48,441 --> 00:12:53,300
would relieve his muscles, and he could develop them and learn to walk.

175
00:12:54,180 --> 00:12:56,700
The warm water soothed his weary muscles,

176
00:12:56,701 --> 00:13:00,560
and he found comfort in the company of others who shared his affliction.

177
00:13:01,520 --> 00:13:04,180
So enamored was Roosevelt with Warm Springs

178
00:13:04,181 --> 00:13:07,520
that in 1927, he decided to buy it.

179
00:13:07,700 --> 00:13:13,940
But a polio resort would never draw healthy patrons to waters used by polio patients.

180
00:13:15,680 --> 00:13:21,840
Warm Springs was a therapeutic success, but as a resort, it was a financial disaster.

181
00:13:23,700 --> 00:13:27,600
The next few years, Roosevelt worked in his law office part-time,

182
00:13:27,860 --> 00:13:30,520
but spent most of his time searching for a cure,

183
00:13:31,020 --> 00:13:34,400
working futilely to recover the strength of his paralyzed limbs.

184
00:13:37,700 --> 00:13:42,760
Roosevelt's convalescence was long, but his return to public life was triumphant.

185
00:13:42,761 --> 00:13:47,720
A familiar figure, now on crutches, slowly mounted the rostrum.

186
00:13:48,560 --> 00:13:51,500
He was clearly an heroic figure to the delegates.

187
00:13:52,720 --> 00:13:58,220
Easily elected governor of New York, he had emerged from his trial with conviction and strength,

188
00:13:58,620 --> 00:14:02,840
prepared to lead a country headed for its own dark trials of confidence.

189
00:14:02,841 --> 00:14:10,720
had warm springs been even a modest success the story of polio might have been different

190
00:14:10,721 --> 00:14:17,480
but by 1928 roosevelt had spent almost 200 000 nearly two-thirds of his family fortune

191
00:14:17,481 --> 00:14:19,660
trying to keep the resort afloat

192
00:14:19,661 --> 00:14:27,940
the unlikely man who would find the solution was roosevelt's friend and law partner basil o'connor

193
00:14:28,740 --> 00:14:33,880
The son of Irish immigrants, O'Connor had worked his way up from the ghettos of New York.

194
00:14:34,300 --> 00:14:37,840
He was a no-nonsense leader, gruff but compassionate.

195
00:14:39,180 --> 00:14:43,880
Basil O'Connor was a bulldog once he got onto an idea.

196
00:14:44,880 --> 00:14:49,740
He was tough. He was soft-hearted, too. He didn't like to fire people.

197
00:14:49,880 --> 00:14:54,360
He remembered that his father had once been fired and saw his father come home crying.

198
00:14:54,361 --> 00:14:59,180
So he had a lot of compassion, but he was also a hard-boiled kind of guy.

199
00:14:59,780 --> 00:15:04,900
As Roosevelt built his political base, it was O'Connor he turned to to get things done.

200
00:15:05,660 --> 00:15:07,660
He liked O'Connor and trusted him.

201
00:15:08,240 --> 00:15:13,780
O'Connor was the logical person to turn to when Warm Springs was on the brink of financial ruin.

202
00:15:15,080 --> 00:15:19,100
Basil O'Connor reluctantly went down to Warm Springs with Roosevelt,

203
00:15:19,240 --> 00:15:23,340
and Roosevelt said, come on, and he wasn't too enthusiastic about the whole idea,

204
00:15:23,341 --> 00:15:27,640
didn't give a darn about polio or polio victims,

205
00:15:27,760 --> 00:15:31,640
but he agreed to do it and gradually got more interested.

206
00:15:32,160 --> 00:15:36,140
And eventually he gave up his law practice and made it a crusade.

207
00:15:36,700 --> 00:15:40,160
As O'Connor thought about what might be done with Warm Springs,

208
00:15:40,620 --> 00:15:43,320
Roosevelt's political skills were propelling him forward.

209
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:48,220
By 1932, Roosevelt had reached the highest office in the land.

210
00:15:49,400 --> 00:15:50,780
Gripped by the Great Depression,

211
00:15:50,781 --> 00:15:54,380
America had a president that had experience with hardship.

212
00:15:56,080 --> 00:15:58,280
Roosevelt understood fear and uncertainty

213
00:15:58,281 --> 00:16:01,440
and he understood the power of the presidency.

214
00:16:02,560 --> 00:16:05,400
Roosevelt was quick to get on the radio

215
00:16:05,401 --> 00:16:08,840
in what he called fireside chats and reassure the country.

216
00:16:08,980 --> 00:16:10,000
There was his famous phrase,

217
00:16:10,620 --> 00:16:12,720
we have nothing to fear but fear itself.

218
00:16:13,320 --> 00:16:14,820
He sold the country on that.

219
00:16:14,940 --> 00:16:18,920
Conditions didn't improve terribly much until World War II

220
00:16:18,921 --> 00:16:23,600
and with huge government spending on war production and so on.

221
00:16:24,080 --> 00:16:28,040
That really ended the Depression, but Roosevelt ended it in people's minds.

222
00:16:29,480 --> 00:16:33,180
Roosevelt's manipulation of the media would help him wage a great battle

223
00:16:33,181 --> 00:16:35,960
against the disease he felt compelled to hide.

224
00:16:37,040 --> 00:16:42,320
Mainly it was just a general gentleman's agreement among the media and so on

225
00:16:42,321 --> 00:16:45,480
that you didn't photograph the president in this way.

226
00:16:45,481 --> 00:16:49,340
so the country as a whole did not realize that he was crippled.

227
00:16:50,500 --> 00:16:54,160
Newspapers, radio, and film were helping America shape its opinions,

228
00:16:54,540 --> 00:16:59,960
and Roosevelt was about to use his political clout to launch a great crusade against polio.

229
00:17:01,920 --> 00:17:03,980
Roosevelt and O'Connor had an idea.

230
00:17:04,600 --> 00:17:08,280
Warm Springs could be saved by turning it into a non-profit organization

231
00:17:08,281 --> 00:17:11,700
called the National Foundation for Infantile Paralysis.

232
00:17:12,060 --> 00:17:15,380
The foundation would provide money to help the afflicted

233
00:17:15,381 --> 00:17:17,560
and fund the search for a vaccine.

234
00:17:18,400 --> 00:17:22,180
To raise money, Roosevelt sponsored a presidential ball

235
00:17:22,181 --> 00:17:25,940
and invited political appointees from around the country to attend.

236
00:17:27,340 --> 00:17:30,540
The birthday balls were a big success all over the country.

237
00:17:30,660 --> 00:17:33,260
They started to raise about a million dollars a year,

238
00:17:33,960 --> 00:17:36,880
and they didn't quite know what to do with all that money,

239
00:17:37,180 --> 00:17:40,380
so they began to establish local chapters all over the country.

240
00:17:40,780 --> 00:17:43,600
But the foundation's coffers would stay filled

241
00:17:43,601 --> 00:17:45,720
only so long as Roosevelt was president.

242
00:17:46,340 --> 00:17:49,420
A longer-term solution to the fundraising dilemma was needed.

243
00:17:57,880 --> 00:18:00,760
Advertising jingles were beginning to help sell toothpaste.

244
00:18:01,240 --> 00:18:03,120
Roosevelt and O'Connor had an idea.

245
00:18:03,680 --> 00:18:06,100
Maybe they could find a way to convince America

246
00:18:06,101 --> 00:18:08,420
to fund the drive for a polio cure.

247
00:18:09,400 --> 00:18:11,120
Hollywood was anxious to help.

248
00:18:11,640 --> 00:18:13,500
All that was needed was a slogan.

249
00:18:13,600 --> 00:18:21,520
So in 1937, the National Foundation for Infantile Paralysis was formed, and they had a meeting.

250
00:18:22,700 --> 00:18:28,800
And Eddie Cantor was there, and he said, why not have people send dimes to the White House?

251
00:18:29,480 --> 00:18:32,220
And maybe we can call it the March of Dimes.

252
00:18:33,560 --> 00:18:36,560
Oh, I know. That's a March of Dimes, the Infantile Paralysis Fund.

253
00:18:36,680 --> 00:18:37,380
That's right.

254
00:18:37,381 --> 00:18:40,360
And Judy, when I spend a dime on myself for some little luxury like this,

255
00:18:40,361 --> 00:18:44,600
I always think about those unfortunate kids and how far just a dime will go toward helping.

256
00:18:45,540 --> 00:18:49,240
At first, Roosevelt and O'Connor thought their campaign had failed.

257
00:18:49,740 --> 00:18:51,420
Only a few dimes trickled in.

258
00:18:51,720 --> 00:18:54,260
But soon, the trickle became a torrent.

259
00:18:55,040 --> 00:19:00,520
So overwhelmed was the mailroom at the White House that important correspondents couldn't get through.

260
00:19:01,580 --> 00:19:09,720
The money poured in quickly, but a scientific tragedy not linked to the foundation showed the need to proceed slowly.

261
00:19:10,360 --> 00:19:18,280
A couple of scientists developed an inactivated virus polio vaccine and tried it, and it caused some deaths.

262
00:19:18,281 --> 00:19:22,040
The vaccine, there was a weakened polio virus, it wasn't weakened enough,

263
00:19:22,460 --> 00:19:32,220
but this made O'Connor and others see the need for a major research program that would hopefully lead to a successful vaccine.

264
00:19:32,960 --> 00:19:36,280
Two children had died and three had become paralyzed.

265
00:19:36,281 --> 00:19:41,280
O'Connor acted quickly, establishing a vaccination committee.

266
00:19:41,281 --> 00:19:47,280
Scientists would be screened and chosen carefully before receiving the endorsement of the foundation.

267
00:19:47,281 --> 00:19:51,280
Information would be shared amongst the scientists.

268
00:19:51,281 --> 00:19:57,280
Watching anxiously was a young, virtually unknown doctor named Jonas Salk.

269
00:19:57,281 --> 00:20:03,280
He was about to embark on a journey that would change the course of scientific history.

270
00:20:06,280 --> 00:20:13,200
As the 1940s came to a close, America had weathered the devastating trial of World War II.

271
00:20:16,780 --> 00:20:23,860
The enemy overseas had been vanquished, but the enemy at home, polio, was still just as destructive as ever.

272
00:20:24,700 --> 00:20:29,240
Almost 300,000 cases had been reported since 1916.

273
00:20:29,800 --> 00:20:32,800
Of those, almost 50,000 had died.

274
00:20:36,280 --> 00:20:42,280
Desperate measures were taken to prevent polio spread.

275
00:20:42,281 --> 00:20:49,280
Literally tons of DDT are used on this dread disease that attacks our young.

276
00:20:49,281 --> 00:20:53,280
Parents wanted freedom from the fear that their babies could suddenly be stricken,

277
00:20:53,281 --> 00:20:58,280
but progress on a vaccine was slow.

278
00:20:58,281 --> 00:21:04,280
Scientist Albert Sabin had been working to find the solution since the mid-30s.

279
00:21:04,281 --> 00:21:11,280
Like Jenner and Pasteur, Sabin believed that weakening the live virus was the key.

280
00:21:11,281 --> 00:21:20,280
Sabin would one day help eradicate polio, but his early work led to the mistaken belief that polio virus grew only in spinal tissue.

281
00:21:20,281 --> 00:21:26,280
It meant that large quantities of the virus could never be harvested since spinal tissue was unstable.

282
00:21:26,281 --> 00:21:29,280
This was the greatest barrier to a vaccine.

283
00:21:30,280 --> 00:21:34,540
But within a few years, one man, Jonas Salk, would hatch a brilliant plan,

284
00:21:34,900 --> 00:21:39,500
one based on solid science, but born of inspiration and hard work.

285
00:21:40,420 --> 00:21:45,680
The son of Russian Jewish immigrants, Salk had graduated top in his class at college.

286
00:21:46,500 --> 00:21:52,440
With a loan and $1,000 that his parents had scraped together, he entered medical school at NYU.

287
00:21:53,360 --> 00:21:58,100
Ambitious and talented, Salk could look forward to a promising and lucrative career as a physician.

288
00:21:59,280 --> 00:22:07,520
Young doctors in training witnessed first-hand the suffering of polio patients, but Salk

289
00:22:07,521 --> 00:22:13,120
could also hear the hopeful prediction that a new field, virology, might soon solve the

290
00:22:13,121 --> 00:22:15,280
polio puzzle.

291
00:22:15,281 --> 00:22:19,160
Inspired, Salk opted to enter the research field.

292
00:22:19,161 --> 00:22:24,840
When a professor asked him why he'd turned down a lucrative career, Salk replied, there's

293
00:22:24,841 --> 00:22:29,160
more to life than money.

294
00:22:29,161 --> 00:22:33,240
Upon graduation, he began looking for a research home to start his exploration.

295
00:22:34,060 --> 00:22:37,300
He found it at the University of Michigan with Thomas Francis,

296
00:22:37,760 --> 00:22:40,020
who was experimenting with the flu virus.

297
00:22:41,220 --> 00:22:44,300
Francis was experimenting with a new approach for vaccines.

298
00:22:45,020 --> 00:22:48,460
Instead of weakening the virus, as Pasteur and Sabin had done,

299
00:22:48,461 --> 00:22:52,700
he killed the inside of the virus, the part that allowed it to replicate.

300
00:22:53,580 --> 00:22:57,460
The outer shell could still fool the body into believing it was infected

301
00:22:57,461 --> 00:22:59,520
and thus fight the disease.

302
00:23:00,740 --> 00:23:03,020
France's work was made possible in part

303
00:23:03,021 --> 00:23:06,900
because by 1935, the electron microscope

304
00:23:06,901 --> 00:23:09,760
provided the first look at a virus.

305
00:23:10,520 --> 00:23:15,940
A virus is basically a package of genetic information.

306
00:23:16,320 --> 00:23:19,980
You could compare it to an audio tape or a video tape

307
00:23:19,981 --> 00:23:23,360
that carries information for making sound

308
00:23:23,361 --> 00:23:26,820
or making pictures, but without a machine to play it,

309
00:23:26,821 --> 00:23:29,120
the tape itself is inert.

310
00:23:29,860 --> 00:23:33,780
Healthy cells in the human body are the machine that play the polio tape.

311
00:23:34,300 --> 00:23:38,260
The proteins on the outer shell of the virus bind to healthy cells.

312
00:23:38,940 --> 00:23:46,200
The inside of the virus, the RNA or DNA, allows the virus to mutate or grow inside the healthy cell,

313
00:23:46,460 --> 00:23:47,960
taking over the healthy cell.

314
00:23:48,520 --> 00:23:51,920
The human body provides natural protection called antibodies,

315
00:23:51,921 --> 00:23:56,780
whose purpose is to attack the virus and virus-infected cells and kill them.

316
00:23:56,820 --> 00:24:01,000
but sometimes the virus is too strong and the immune system is overwhelmed.

317
00:24:02,100 --> 00:24:04,160
As Salk quietly worked in the lab,

318
00:24:04,380 --> 00:24:08,960
Basil O'Connor was busy building the foundation into a powerful organization.

319
00:24:10,360 --> 00:24:14,100
As a fashion show sparks the polio drive at the Waldorf,

320
00:24:14,760 --> 00:24:16,920
the models lead the parade of dime donors,

321
00:24:17,440 --> 00:24:20,340
and that iron lung really rings with the silver shower,

322
00:24:20,680 --> 00:24:23,760
which will go far to help infantile paralysis sufferers.

323
00:24:23,761 --> 00:24:29,160
The war years had been tough, but donations continued to pour in, and the

324
00:24:29,161 --> 00:24:32,640
public wanted to know how their money was being spent.

325
00:24:32,641 --> 00:24:37,800
Unfortunately, O'Connor and the Foundation had little to report.

326
00:24:37,801 --> 00:24:43,640
In 1946, the discovery that poor children were less likely to contract polio

327
00:24:43,641 --> 00:24:47,520
explained polio's sudden destructiveness in the 20th century.

328
00:24:47,521 --> 00:24:53,700
When you clean up a country and have modern sanitation, then the infants

329
00:24:53,701 --> 00:24:55,400
and children don't develop immunity,

330
00:24:56,020 --> 00:24:59,740
and bango, at some point, the virus strikes.

331
00:25:06,340 --> 00:25:10,420
By 1947, Salk was anxious for his own lab.

332
00:25:10,421 --> 00:25:13,200
He decided on the University of Pittsburgh,

333
00:25:13,820 --> 00:25:17,400
choosing a place where he could explore his own scientific passions.

334
00:25:18,420 --> 00:25:20,760
Salk went to the University of Pittsburgh

335
00:25:20,761 --> 00:25:23,000
because he said there was nothing there

336
00:25:23,001 --> 00:25:25,320
in the way of virology.

337
00:25:25,720 --> 00:25:29,660
He said going to Pittsburgh was like going west to the frontier days.

338
00:25:30,100 --> 00:25:31,240
Pittsburgh was the frontier.

339
00:25:32,080 --> 00:25:35,920
And he established his own virus laboratory where he could be in charge.

340
00:25:36,800 --> 00:25:40,240
At Pittsburgh, Salk lost no time getting to work.

341
00:25:40,760 --> 00:25:43,720
Once he had put together his lab, virtually from scratch,

342
00:25:43,721 --> 00:25:47,900
he set about the difficult task of raising money for his new project.

343
00:25:48,340 --> 00:25:50,560
It was the foundation to which he turned.

344
00:25:50,561 --> 00:25:55,760
It was known that there was a polio virus, but nobody knew how many types there were

345
00:25:55,761 --> 00:25:57,580
or what they did.

346
00:25:57,581 --> 00:26:03,920
And the National Foundation commissioned Salk to work on typing the virus.

347
00:26:03,921 --> 00:26:08,320
It's important to identify the different strains of the virus because the immune response

348
00:26:08,321 --> 00:26:13,020
of the body can be different against different strains.

349
00:26:13,021 --> 00:26:19,180
In the late 40s, scientists working at Harvard changed the face of polio research.

350
00:26:19,181 --> 00:26:26,420
The highly respected virologist, John Enders, brought down the one great barrier to a vaccine.

351
00:26:26,421 --> 00:26:30,740
Enders and his team were cultivating mumps virus in tissue cultures.

352
00:26:30,741 --> 00:26:35,620
They decided to add a strain of polio to some leftover cultures.

353
00:26:35,621 --> 00:26:37,780
The results were astonishing.

354
00:26:37,781 --> 00:26:41,040
Not only did the virus grow, it thrived.

355
00:26:41,041 --> 00:26:46,180
In one experiment, it was proven that polio could in fact be grown in non-nervous cell

356
00:26:46,181 --> 00:26:47,180
tissue.

357
00:26:47,181 --> 00:26:49,960
This was the plow that broke the plains.

358
00:26:50,460 --> 00:26:54,720
When Salk heard of Ender's 1949 work,

359
00:26:54,721 --> 00:26:58,760
he immediately saw that a vaccine would be possible,

360
00:26:59,040 --> 00:27:00,920
and he got ready to work on one.

361
00:27:01,320 --> 00:27:03,540
The question asked was which was better,

362
00:27:03,920 --> 00:27:06,180
a weakened vaccine, as Sabin favored,

363
00:27:06,460 --> 00:27:09,200
or a killed vaccine, as Salk advocated.

364
00:27:10,460 --> 00:27:13,940
Salk would need to convince O'Connor and the Foundation to support him,

365
00:27:14,240 --> 00:27:17,080
and in 1951, he got his chance.

366
00:27:17,180 --> 00:27:22,420
An invitation to address other working scientists at a conference in Copenhagen.

367
00:27:22,760 --> 00:27:26,440
But it was the trip home that proved almost as valuable as the conference.

368
00:27:27,140 --> 00:27:29,660
Salk returned to America aboard the Queen Mary.

369
00:27:30,020 --> 00:27:32,840
Also aboard were Basil O'Connor and his wife.

370
00:27:33,580 --> 00:27:36,280
O'Connor invited them to sit at his table.

371
00:27:37,400 --> 00:27:42,920
And Salk was somewhat entranced by this smooth, sophisticated guy, O'Connor.

372
00:27:42,921 --> 00:27:48,360
and O'Connor was taken with this young, enthusiastic, serious kid

373
00:27:48,361 --> 00:27:50,540
who believed that a vaccine was possible.

374
00:27:51,240 --> 00:27:52,960
It was the beginning of a beautiful friendship.

375
00:27:53,880 --> 00:27:56,840
But what Salk was proposing was not free from risk.

376
00:27:57,240 --> 00:27:59,600
O'Connor still remembered 1935,

377
00:28:00,020 --> 00:28:04,460
the last time a killed virus had been used with devastating results.

378
00:28:05,240 --> 00:28:08,360
Salk relied on formalin to kill the inner core of the virus.

379
00:28:08,361 --> 00:28:10,940
It was the exact same method that had been used

380
00:28:10,941 --> 00:28:14,440
during the ill-fated experiment in 1935.

381
00:28:14,441 --> 00:28:17,640
This time, however, Salk believed he knew how to kill

382
00:28:17,641 --> 00:28:20,680
the virus completely.

383
00:28:20,681 --> 00:28:23,680
Despite the risk, O'Connor trusted Salk.

384
00:28:23,681 --> 00:28:27,760
The two men, as different as they seemed, had much in common.

385
00:28:27,761 --> 00:28:30,700
Both were sons of immigrants, self-made,

386
00:28:30,701 --> 00:28:33,200
hardworking, determined.

387
00:28:33,201 --> 00:28:37,560
And they shared a common passion to rid the world of polio.

388
00:28:37,561 --> 00:28:40,500
While O'Connor was interested in Salk and his work,

389
00:28:40,501 --> 00:28:43,440
There were other scientists competing for the Foundation's funds.

390
00:28:44,140 --> 00:28:52,060
The enormous expense of testing a vaccine meant the Foundation would have to be careful about backing one scientist or approach prematurely.

391
00:28:52,440 --> 00:28:54,660
A recent scandal had proven the point.

392
00:28:55,300 --> 00:29:00,940
Noted scientist Hilary Koprowski shocked the scientific community in 1951,

393
00:29:01,280 --> 00:29:05,600
announcing that he had, on his own, injected live virus into volunteers.

394
00:29:06,360 --> 00:29:12,200
Doubly shocking was the revelation that the volunteers had been patients at a New York mental institution.

395
00:29:13,080 --> 00:29:15,980
No one was infected, but the foundation was outraged.

396
00:29:16,940 --> 00:29:19,580
Salk was certain his killed vaccine was the key.

397
00:29:20,060 --> 00:29:23,940
But important work remained in the lab to prove the vaccine was ready for the public.

398
00:29:24,480 --> 00:29:29,020
Salk focused on the strongest, most virulent strain of polio, the Mahoney strain.

399
00:29:29,180 --> 00:29:33,020
He reasoned that since the virus was dead, strength wouldn't matter.

400
00:29:33,021 --> 00:29:36,560
Besides, the stronger strain grew well in the laboratory.

401
00:29:37,160 --> 00:29:40,460
The antibody response would be the same in weaker strains.

402
00:29:42,340 --> 00:29:45,340
Salk was making excellent progress, and it was a good thing,

403
00:29:45,660 --> 00:29:50,260
because in 1952, the U.S. was experiencing its worst epidemic yet,

404
00:29:50,800 --> 00:29:56,760
56,000 cases of poliomyelitis, one severe case for every 3,000 people.

405
00:29:57,140 --> 00:29:59,820
The public cried desperately for a solution.

406
00:30:00,540 --> 00:30:03,440
Jonas Salk believed that he could provide it.

407
00:30:09,780 --> 00:30:15,200
By the 1950s, polio's grip over America was the strongest it had ever been.

408
00:30:15,860 --> 00:30:18,600
Polio had become a summer ritual of fear.

409
00:30:19,180 --> 00:30:21,300
But Jonas Salk was about to change that.

410
00:30:21,660 --> 00:30:24,820
For five years, Salk had been working feverishly in the lab,

411
00:30:25,180 --> 00:30:28,620
conducting tests to prove that his vaccine could work.

412
00:30:29,280 --> 00:30:33,800
For those beyond a cure, the March of Dimes did what could be done to ease the pain.

413
00:30:34,480 --> 00:30:39,700
Polio was an expensive disease, and many turned to the March of Dimes for help.

414
00:30:40,800 --> 00:30:46,440
Back in the 50s, my shoes cost between $75 and $100.

415
00:30:47,180 --> 00:30:53,240
My mother didn't have to purchase the brace for me because she petitioned the March of Dimes,

416
00:30:53,241 --> 00:30:59,140
and the March of Dimes provided the money for the brace.

417
00:30:59,720 --> 00:31:01,940
But she had to pay for the shoes.

418
00:31:03,820 --> 00:31:06,460
Rehabilitation required money and patience.

419
00:31:06,820 --> 00:31:10,420
The children and I were told how to walk.

420
00:31:11,320 --> 00:31:17,620
And it was the right arm, left foot, left arm, right foot.

421
00:31:17,720 --> 00:31:20,280
And you know, you had to have this rhythm going when you walked.

422
00:31:20,281 --> 00:31:22,800
You couldn't just walk any kind of way.

423
00:31:23,240 --> 00:31:29,040
With each epidemic, the number of polio patients requiring expensive medical treatment increased.

424
00:31:29,540 --> 00:31:32,560
It seemed that there was no end in sight to the tragedy.

425
00:31:33,560 --> 00:31:38,960
After five long years working in the lab, Salk was ready for the next momentous step,

426
00:31:39,420 --> 00:31:41,840
trying the vaccine on a human being.

427
00:31:42,600 --> 00:31:49,740
In a bold move, he injected not only himself, but his entire family with his killed polio virus.

428
00:31:49,741 --> 00:31:56,280
I think he was confident about the safety of the vaccine, and he was willing to inoculate

429
00:31:56,281 --> 00:32:01,120
himself and his children as an indication of his confidence in the safety of it.

430
00:32:01,121 --> 00:32:02,580
The injections worked.

431
00:32:02,581 --> 00:32:06,960
Salk and his family showed evidence of antibodies that could fight

432
00:32:06,961 --> 00:32:12,630
polio. But it was far from the proof needed to convince the scientific community.

433
00:32:12,631 --> 00:32:13,331
For that,

434
00:32:13,332 --> 00:32:15,494
a large trial was necessary.

435
00:32:15,495 --> 00:32:20,760
First, Salk decided to conduct a top-secret test on a small number

436
00:32:20,761 --> 00:32:24,409
of polio patients at the Watson Home for Crippled Children.

437
00:32:24,410 --> 00:32:26,760
Despite his confidence in the vaccine,

438
00:32:26,761 --> 00:32:31,051
Salk had to take every precaution possible. Dr.

439
00:32:31,052 --> 00:32:35,160
Salk felt that it would be more acceptable to

440
00:32:35,161 --> 00:32:41,760
have a vaccine used in people who had already been exposed to the virus than it would be to

441
00:32:41,761 --> 00:32:45,600
use that vaccine in people who had not been exposed.

442
00:32:45,601 --> 00:32:49,240
When the results were in, antibodies had increased.

443
00:32:49,241 --> 00:32:52,740
The experiment had worked.

444
00:32:52,741 --> 00:32:55,840
But would the vaccine work on healthy children?

445
00:32:55,841 --> 00:32:58,480
Would it provide significant protection?

446
00:32:58,481 --> 00:33:00,780
Could healthy kids become infected?

447
00:33:00,781 --> 00:33:03,180
These were all possibilities.

448
00:33:03,181 --> 00:33:09,060
Some scientists didn't think the larger trials should proceed.

449
00:33:09,061 --> 00:33:14,660
There's a lot of debate about when vaccines would be tried and who would take them.

450
00:33:15,080 --> 00:33:19,640
There's a consideration of cost and who should pay for the vaccine,

451
00:33:19,900 --> 00:33:24,000
who should be given the vaccine, how broadly it should be used, and so on.

452
00:33:24,800 --> 00:33:28,660
But Salk, with O'Connor's support, called for an immediate trial.

453
00:33:28,661 --> 00:33:30,460
There was no time to waste.

454
00:33:31,980 --> 00:33:34,380
Any vaccine that is available should be used.

455
00:33:34,381 --> 00:33:39,660
And whether a vaccine is available, it should be used now and should be used before this coming summer.

456
00:33:40,260 --> 00:33:45,160
For a large trial, hundreds of thousands of people would need to be convinced to participate.

457
00:33:45,880 --> 00:33:47,900
Parents would be volunteering their children.

458
00:33:48,900 --> 00:33:53,640
Salk found himself playing a new role now, public spokesperson for his vaccine.

459
00:33:54,580 --> 00:33:55,820
And the press loved him.

460
00:33:56,260 --> 00:33:58,980
Jonas Salk was direct, sincere, and intelligent.

461
00:33:58,981 --> 00:34:01,680
He inspired confidence and trust.

462
00:34:01,681 --> 00:34:05,720
the perfect hero to soothe an anxious, expectant public.

463
00:34:06,360 --> 00:34:10,140
But Salk embraced his growing fame with great reluctance.

464
00:34:11,000 --> 00:34:15,240
Some scientists were outraged at the increased attention paid to Salk.

465
00:34:15,760 --> 00:34:21,400
His willingness to mention the contribution of others did little to mollify the scientific community.

466
00:34:22,100 --> 00:34:26,180
Salk later said that Enders threw a forward pass and I caught it.

467
00:34:26,500 --> 00:34:30,960
And other scientists never forgave Salk for taking that much credit.

468
00:34:31,680 --> 00:34:35,420
Despite the heavy and growing responsibilities in public,

469
00:34:35,421 --> 00:34:37,460
Salk worked privately in the lab

470
00:34:37,461 --> 00:34:40,960
to ensure all details were in place.

471
00:34:40,961 --> 00:34:43,660
The last most important hurdle was to ensure

472
00:34:43,661 --> 00:34:47,560
mass production of his vaccine was done properly.

473
00:34:47,561 --> 00:34:50,640
Big commercial labs would manufacture large quantities

474
00:34:50,641 --> 00:34:53,340
of the vaccine in assembly line fashion.

475
00:34:53,341 --> 00:34:56,600
But the slightest mistake could be devastating.

476
00:34:56,601 --> 00:34:59,880
A bad vaccine going out would be disastrous.

477
00:34:59,881 --> 00:35:01,920
Production had to be perfect.

478
00:35:04,860 --> 00:35:07,220
Basil O'Connor was also taking steps

479
00:35:07,221 --> 00:35:10,260
to ensure the trial came off perfectly.

480
00:35:10,261 --> 00:35:14,320
There could be no questions about methodology or results.

481
00:35:14,321 --> 00:35:17,240
The foundation would provide the logistical support

482
00:35:17,241 --> 00:35:20,660
and coordinate personnel.

483
00:35:20,661 --> 00:35:22,760
Through the early spring of 53,

484
00:35:22,761 --> 00:35:26,440
the foundation struggled with numerous decisions.

485
00:35:26,441 --> 00:35:29,820
Over 500,000 people would be participating.

486
00:35:29,821 --> 00:35:34,260
Boxes of supplies and vial after vial of vaccine would have to be distributed.

487
00:35:35,200 --> 00:35:39,360
Then there was the task of tracking and carefully tabulating the results.

488
00:35:41,060 --> 00:35:46,080
Thomas Francis, Salk's friend and mentor from Ann Arbor, was asked to oversee the trial.

489
00:35:46,640 --> 00:35:50,720
It was a fitting reunion with the man who had helped inspire the Salk vaccine.

490
00:35:51,120 --> 00:35:56,700
By early 1954, two years after Salk had injected himself with the vaccine,

491
00:35:57,220 --> 00:35:59,540
the vaccine was ready to be given to the public.

492
00:35:59,820 --> 00:36:03,660
Salk and O'Connor were convinced the vaccinations would proceed safely,

493
00:36:03,661 --> 00:36:07,000
but Sabin still doubted the wisdom of the trial.

494
00:36:07,001 --> 00:36:15,340
There had never been a trial of the size of the 1954 field trial of the Salk vaccine.

495
00:36:15,341 --> 00:36:22,100
And before that trial was approved by the Public Health Service,

496
00:36:22,101 --> 00:36:27,080
many bitterly opposed it, among them Enders, Sabin.

497
00:36:27,081 --> 00:36:28,160
They thought it was dangerous.

498
00:36:28,540 --> 00:36:30,600
A, it wouldn't work, and B, it was dangerous.

499
00:36:31,120 --> 00:36:33,080
Enders told Salk that he was unethical.

500
00:36:34,120 --> 00:36:37,140
Salk and the foundation believed they had to try.

501
00:36:37,980 --> 00:36:41,780
On April 26, 1954, the trials began.

502
00:36:42,660 --> 00:36:46,500
Children were inoculated by thousands of volunteer nurses and doctors.

503
00:36:47,440 --> 00:36:49,420
They were called polio pioneers

504
00:36:49,421 --> 00:36:54,420
and told by teachers that pioneers are special people who go first.

505
00:36:54,421 --> 00:36:59,120
Chalkboards across America were filled with the same message.

506
00:37:01,820 --> 00:37:05,380
Over 200,000 vials of the vaccine would be needed,

507
00:37:05,780 --> 00:37:08,900
over one million needles, as many cotton swabs,

508
00:37:09,260 --> 00:37:12,900
not to mention the public space required for such an undertaking.

509
00:37:14,300 --> 00:37:18,360
First, blood was taken from each child, then the inoculation given.

510
00:37:19,700 --> 00:37:22,720
Two weeks later, another blood sample was taken

511
00:37:22,721 --> 00:37:25,420
to compare the difference in antibody levels.

512
00:37:26,280 --> 00:37:28,560
All the samples had to be checked carefully.

513
00:37:29,240 --> 00:37:33,480
With over 500,000 participants, the logistics were enormous.

514
00:37:34,860 --> 00:37:36,680
At first, everything went well.

515
00:37:37,280 --> 00:37:38,920
Then the unimaginable happened.

516
00:37:38,921 --> 00:37:43,720
A child inoculated by the Salk vaccine had come down with polio.

517
00:37:44,440 --> 00:37:46,680
It was a tragedy that could end the trials.

518
00:37:47,260 --> 00:37:50,180
Even worse, the media got hold of the news,

519
00:37:50,181 --> 00:37:52,940
releasing it to a startled, worried public.

520
00:37:54,240 --> 00:37:55,320
Salk was crushed.

521
00:37:55,980 --> 00:37:58,800
O'Connor told friends later Salk was heartbroken.

522
00:37:59,300 --> 00:38:04,200
It was his vaccine, and he knew there wasn't a damn thing wrong with it when it was made properly.

523
00:38:05,160 --> 00:38:07,380
Fortunately, the disaster was soon explained.

524
00:38:08,240 --> 00:38:11,120
One of the labs had failed to kill the virus completely.

525
00:38:11,840 --> 00:38:15,140
Salk reassured the public that it would never happen again.

526
00:38:15,141 --> 00:38:26,493
Our contention in the thing that we discussed today has been that it should be possible to prevent paralytic poliomyelitis by the proper use of a properly constituted

527
00:38:26,494 --> 00:38:27,194
vaccine.

528
00:38:27,780 --> 00:38:33,540
Despite their fears, most parents were desperate for a cure, and they agreed to continue the trial.

529
00:38:34,300 --> 00:38:37,280
Most believed that the benefits outweighed the risk.

530
00:38:38,420 --> 00:38:43,580
In the scientific community, and particularly in medicine, people like to move slowly.

531
00:38:43,581 --> 00:38:46,280
You know, there's a saying, first do no harm.

532
00:38:46,700 --> 00:38:47,900
That isn't really possible.

533
00:38:48,440 --> 00:38:50,340
You can't do anything without doing some harm.

534
00:38:50,820 --> 00:38:56,340
By March of 1955, all the lab work had been completed and the results tabulated.

535
00:38:57,000 --> 00:39:00,480
An anxious and expectant nation waited for the results.

536
00:39:01,380 --> 00:39:04,640
A conference was called on April 12, 1955,

537
00:39:05,340 --> 00:39:08,820
coincidentally the 10th anniversary of Franklin Roosevelt's death.

538
00:39:08,821 --> 00:39:13,800
I was a reporter on the Minneapolis Tribune in 1955.

539
00:39:14,600 --> 00:39:17,100
We had had several bad polio epidemics.

540
00:39:17,660 --> 00:39:21,660
We were frantic for this news, and the PR people and the guards

541
00:39:21,661 --> 00:39:25,840
brought the stacks of press releases into the press room,

542
00:39:25,940 --> 00:39:28,560
and we all surged forward to get them, you know,

543
00:39:28,600 --> 00:39:33,140
like a bunch of shoppers at Macy's during a sale, every man for himself.

544
00:39:33,880 --> 00:39:37,080
Soon the word was out the Salk vaccine worked.

545
00:39:37,081 --> 00:39:45,480
A nation gripped by fear summer after summer was able to breathe a heavy, happy sigh of relief as Salk shared the news.

546
00:39:46,140 --> 00:39:50,360
Asked by the media who owned the patent of the vaccine, Salk replied,

547
00:39:50,720 --> 00:39:54,400
The people do. There is no patent. Could you patent the sun?

548
00:39:55,420 --> 00:40:02,540
Salk's vaccine proved that polio could be beaten, but the task to end the scourge in America was far from accomplished.

549
00:40:03,280 --> 00:40:07,480
As news of the Salk vaccine was reaching a hungry, anxious public,

550
00:40:07,900 --> 00:40:10,420
Albert Sabin was working on his own vaccine.

551
00:40:11,260 --> 00:40:16,460
Sabin remained convinced that a live virus approach was better than the Salk vaccine.

552
00:40:17,380 --> 00:40:23,420
His work was about to profoundly influence the challenge to eradicate polio from the world.

553
00:40:23,421 --> 00:40:35,740
By 1957, millions of children had been vaccinated with Salk's vaccine for polio.

554
00:40:36,440 --> 00:40:42,740
It was clear that although Salk's vaccine dealt polio a lethal blow, it couldn't finish the job.

555
00:40:43,240 --> 00:40:46,740
His vaccine required multiple shots to maintain its effectiveness.

556
00:40:47,680 --> 00:40:52,560
Unbelievably, some had to be convinced to take the follow-up shots that were required.

557
00:40:53,420 --> 00:40:57,420
Albert Sabin believed he had found a solution to the problem.

558
00:40:57,421 --> 00:41:04,420
He had successfully weakened the virus, and he was convinced his live virus method was better than Salk's.

559
00:41:04,421 --> 00:41:09,420
It could be taken orally and was therefore much more convenient.

560
00:41:09,421 --> 00:41:14,420
Although it required refrigeration, it was much easier to administer on a mass basis.

561
00:41:14,421 --> 00:41:19,420
No matter where people lived, they could easily take the vaccine.

562
00:41:19,421 --> 00:41:23,940
Those giving the Sabin vaccine needed no special medical training.

563
00:41:24,820 --> 00:41:28,820
Best of all, children were grateful to be spared the nasty needle.

564
00:41:31,220 --> 00:41:41,880
In the late 1950s, in an unusual example of detente, the Sabin vaccine was sent to the Soviet Union in Eastern Europe and given to hundreds of thousands of children.

565
00:41:41,881 --> 00:41:47,820
Ostensibly used to test the effectiveness of Sabin's vaccine, the Eastern European trials

566
00:41:47,821 --> 00:41:53,100
were so successful that the Sabin approach became the vaccine of choice the world over.

567
00:41:54,100 --> 00:41:58,960
In fact, it was Sabin's vaccine that would ultimately eradicate polio in the Western

568
00:41:58,961 --> 00:42:02,500
Hemisphere, although it would take decades to do so.

569
00:42:04,260 --> 00:42:08,660
The last reported case was in Peru as recently as 1992.

570
00:42:08,661 --> 00:42:14,500
Today, polio continues to cripple children in developing nations, but with the help of

571
00:42:14,501 --> 00:42:20,100
Rotary International, the Pan American Health Organization plans to eradicate polio from

572
00:42:20,101 --> 00:42:23,500
the world by the year 2000.

573
00:42:23,501 --> 00:42:29,780
It's important to consider also that the work of Semi and Salk is not yet finished.

574
00:42:29,781 --> 00:42:35,380
They gave us the tools, but we are not using yet to its full power.

575
00:42:35,381 --> 00:42:42,020
And I believe that if there is collaboration between all countries, if there is support

576
00:42:42,021 --> 00:42:47,700
from the industrialized world to the developing countries, we can have a 21st century free

577
00:42:47,701 --> 00:42:49,760
of poliomyelitis.

578
00:42:49,761 --> 00:42:54,560
Despite the tremendous success of those who work to battle the disease, the fight continues

579
00:42:54,561 --> 00:42:56,160
to this day.

580
00:42:56,161 --> 00:43:00,880
Those stricken with polio as children find that as adults, the disease often returns

581
00:43:00,881 --> 00:43:03,720
to cripple again.

582
00:43:03,721 --> 00:43:10,440
I want people to know that when polio comes back, you go into a deep depression, you go

583
00:43:10,441 --> 00:43:12,620
into denial.

584
00:43:12,621 --> 00:43:20,420
The best thing to do in my life is not to use up too much energy.

585
00:43:20,421 --> 00:43:29,020
Find ways to conserve energy because if I get tired, my tiredness converts changes into

586
00:43:29,021 --> 00:43:31,620
pain.

587
00:43:31,621 --> 00:43:36,320
are still trying to discover why those who suffered as children suffer again in later

588
00:43:36,321 --> 00:43:41,856
years as adults. Jonas Salk had helped eradicate a cruel disease.

589
00:43:41,857 --> 00:43:43,900
He was still a young man

590
00:43:43,901 --> 00:43:46,982
with many years of work and research ahead of him.

591
00:43:46,983 --> 00:43:49,140
With the help of the March of Dimes

592
00:43:49,141 --> 00:43:55,740
and the city of San Diego, Salk established a research institute with a profound and unique

593
00:43:55,741 --> 00:44:00,060
mission, a mission inspired by the work he had done to fight polio.

594
00:44:00,061 --> 00:44:07,560
Dr. Salk had a broad view of his place in human history and human culture.

595
00:44:07,561 --> 00:44:14,820
He felt that as human beings we should be conscious of the many dimensions of our existence.

596
00:44:14,821 --> 00:44:20,300
So he founded an institute that would be devoted to understanding the physical, the mental

597
00:44:20,301 --> 00:44:23,820
and the spiritual aspects of human beings.

598
00:44:23,821 --> 00:44:27,820
To the very end, Salk approached his work with humility.

599
00:44:27,821 --> 00:44:33,380
He spent his last years struggling to uncover the secrets of another virus that causes terror,

600
00:44:33,381 --> 00:44:36,280
the AIDS virus.

601
00:44:36,281 --> 00:44:39,000
Dr. Salk didn't reminisce too much.

602
00:44:39,001 --> 00:44:43,220
He was very wrapped up in what was happening now.

603
00:44:43,221 --> 00:44:49,760
I recall on one occasion he was asked why he thought there had been such a big impact

604
00:44:49,761 --> 00:44:52,980
of his discovery of a polio vaccine.

605
00:44:52,981 --> 00:44:58,040
And he said that he felt it was because that vaccine lifted a fear from people.

606
00:44:58,041 --> 00:45:05,060
So he understood that a large degree of the impact of the vaccine was to remove fear from

607
00:45:05,061 --> 00:45:07,740
people of the disease of polio.

608
00:45:07,741 --> 00:45:12,520
Jonas Salk communicated his vision through his work, through his words, and by the gift

609
00:45:12,521 --> 00:45:15,660
of freedom from the fear of polio.

610
00:45:15,661 --> 00:45:20,740
Albert Sabin had worked for over four decades to beat polio and could also claim credit

611
00:45:20,741 --> 00:45:23,240
for victory over the disease.

612
00:45:23,241 --> 00:45:28,720
He and Salk, scientific adversaries, had both succeeded in their common goal to bring the

613
00:45:28,721 --> 00:45:31,380
polio terror to an end.

614
00:45:31,381 --> 00:45:35,860
Almost everyone that received the Salk vaccine would ultimately receive the Sabin vaccine

615
00:45:35,861 --> 00:45:39,920
as well, just to be certain they were immunized.

616
00:45:39,921 --> 00:45:46,260
The fear today is of killer viruses that threaten to wipe out entire populations.

617
00:45:46,261 --> 00:45:50,980
The pioneers who worked to unlock the secrets of one of the 20th century's most destructive

618
00:45:50,981 --> 00:45:56,280
viruses, polio, have helped to pave the way for future exploration.

619
00:45:56,281 --> 00:46:01,660
Hopefully the painful lessons of yesterday will help answer the difficult questions of

620
00:46:01,661 --> 00:46:02,361
tomorrow.

621
00:46:45,040 --> 00:46:46,240
I love you.

